\`html/inc/email.inc\`'s \`send_email()\` requires \`PHPMailer.php\` and \`SMTP.php\`, but never \`Exception.php\`, even though all three ship together in the vendored PHPMailer library:

\`\`\`php
if (file_exists("../inc/PHPMailer/src/PHPMailer.php") && file_exists("../inc/PHPMailer/src/SMTP.php")) {
    require_once("../inc/PHPMailer/src/PHPMailer.php");
    require_once("../inc/PHPMailer/src/SMTP.php");
}
\`\`\`

This is invisible on the happy path — \`PHPMailer\PHPMailer\Exception\` is only touched when \`smtpConnect()\`/\`smtpSend()\`/\`postSend()\` hit a real connection or auth failure and try to \`throw new Exception(...)\`. Since that class was never loaded, PHP can't construct it, so instead of a catchable exception that PHPMailer's own code turns into a clean \`$mail->Send()\` returning \`false\`, you get an uncaught fatal \`Error: Class "PHPMailer\PHPMailer\Exception" not found\`, crashing the entire request with a raw stack trace on any project running with \`display_errors\` on.

Reproduced live by pointing a test project's SMTP credentials at an invalid password.

**Fix:**
\`\`\`php
if (file_exists("../inc/PHPMailer/src/PHPMailer.php") && file_exists("../inc/PHPMailer/src/SMTP.php") && file_exists("../inc/PHPMailer/src/Exception.php")) {
    require_once("../inc/PHPMailer/src/Exception.php");
    require_once("../inc/PHPMailer/src/PHPMailer.php");
    require_once("../inc/PHPMailer/src/SMTP.php");
}
\`\`\`

